2. Who Verifi Is For

In one line: Verifi is for elderly people juggling several medications — and it matters most for the ones among them who can't just glance at a label and be sure it's right.

The main group: elderly people managing multiple medications. This is who Verifi is built for, first and foremost. It's a big, well-documented group in Portugal — over half of seniors take several regular medications, and 1 in 4 live alone with no one nearby to double-check them. That's the group Verifi has to win over first, because that's also who a paying care agency or facility will actually be using it on later (see Section 12).

Within that group, some people need it more urgently:

People who can't read the label well (low vision).
People who find medical instructions hard to understand (low health literacy).
People who get confused between similar-looking boxes (cognitive load).

These are not a separate audience — they're the sharpest, most urgent version of the same problem every elderly person managing medication has. Verifi is designed with their needs in mind first (that's why it's voice-first, not just screen-based), but it isn't only for them. Making it only for them would actually be a mistake — it would shrink both the market story and the group of people a paying facility would use it on.

Why not just say "for low-vision/cognitive-load users" as the headline? Because the numbers don't back that up as tightly in Portugal specifically (good data exists on elderly people managing many medications, but not on age-related vision loss here), and the hackathon build doesn't include full accessibility features yet — so leading with that claim would invite a question the demo can't fully answer. The safer, stronger, and still-honest claim is: built for elderly medication management, sharpest for those who struggle to self-check.

Who actually uses it, stage by stage:

Individuals (core — this is what gets built and demoed first)

People managing multiple medications or a complicated schedule.
Elderly users who want a second, trustworthy check.
Within this group: people with low vision, low health literacy, or cognitive load — the ones who benefit most.

This is where trust has to be earned first — before it makes sense to build the caregiver version, and before any business would take it seriously.

Family caregivers (roadmap, not built for the hackathon)

A spouse or adult child helping someone manage medication, with that person's permission.
Wants to know medication was taken, missed, or mismatched — without taking over the whole process.

Professional caregivers and small care facilities (roadmap — this is the actual business)

Home-care aides and small elder-care facilities managing multiple clients per shift.
Their clients are a mix — some low-vision or cognitively impaired, many just elderly people on several prescriptions who want a second check.
Real cost and risk for them today if a mistake happens, which is why they'd realistically pay for this — but only once the individual product has proven itself first (see Sections 10 and 12).

## 3. Core Product Principle

> When the system is confident, simplify the interaction. When it isn't, expose the uncertainty — never guess.

Three states only for any verification: **Confirmed match / Confirmed mismatch / Uncertain.** Uncertain never resolves to a best guess. This is the single idea the whole demo has to prove, and it's the reason the mismatch case has to be shown live, not described.

