## 11. Success Metrics (post-hackathon validation)

- Verification accuracy: false-positive rate (system says match when it's actually a mismatch) must be effectively zero in testing; false "uncertain" is acceptable, false "match" is not.
- Time-to-verify per interaction.
- Verification events per active user per week — the concrete, trackable version of "daily habit," used from the first pilot onward instead of a vague adoption claim.
- Family/caregiver users (once built): reported confidence that a loved one's medication was taken correctly.
- Professional/institutional pilots (once built): reduction in reported medication mismatch incidents per shift/staff member.
- User-reported trust in the system's uncertainty communication (does "I don't know" read as helpful, not broken).

---

## 12. Business Model Summary

- **Primary product:** free or low-cost self-serve tier for individuals and family caregivers — this is where the core experience gets proven and where most users will actually live. Direct consumer payment for health apps is weak in the Portuguese/EU market absent reimbursement, so this tier is about proving the mechanic and building trust, not primary revenue.
- **Business wedge:** home-care agencies and small elder-care facilities (B2B license per caregiver/facility), sold on top of a product individuals already trust, and only pursued once Stage 2 regulatory work (Section 10) is underway or complete — this is a liability-sensitive buyer and will not adopt a non-certified tool at scale.
- **Long-term:** pharmacy and insurer partnerships once safety data accumulates and CE marking is in place; not assumed or built into the MVP or the pilot stage.

